@* Where the browser's own print furniture is turned off.

   The web address and the page number along the foot of a printed page are the browser's
   furniture, not part of the sheet — and no page can turn them off, because the setting
   belongs to whoever is printing. Saying where the switch is takes one line and is the
   difference between a sheet you would hand somebody and one you would not.

   **The band across the top is the sheet's own now, and the switch does not touch it.** The
   `@page` margin box in app.css puts the character's name and the page count there, on every
   page, and Chrome drops its own date-and-title line where a page has filled the slot —
   measured, not assumed. So the sentence names the foot only: the address is what is left of
   the browser's furniture, and the head is not something to tell anyone to turn off.

   **One component because two pages print a sheet**, and the sentence was identical in both. *@

<p class="small muted @Class">
    One page. Turn <b>Headers and footers</b> off under <b>More settings</b> to drop the
    browser's own web-address band; the name and page count across the top are the sheet's.
</p>

